stratification of the "planter" class that own the resources and the servants, along with the laborers who produce (collect and process) the resources. Those who are relatively less capable of producing resources, in which case is women, would specialize in logistical tasks of keeping the colony running instead. As a result, are inferior in status due to dependency.

Classifying colonizers by resources they own upon immigration, those that got "pushed" rather than "pulled" possess effectively no other resource but themselves, therefore must trade it for survival and opportunities. Often, they meet hardship rather than oppurtunities. Nonetheless, to a colony lacking laborers, they are essential.

The case for the South is they require great amount of laborers, as shaped by their economy (determined by $e_0$ and physics of their environment). Slavery became the most efficient option upon the decline of indentured servants. The use of such is expected for the common example of this text of colonization other celestial bodies. Likey, though, robots will fill the need for labor. the occurance of scaling of slave trading seems like a stable equilibrium from its relatively long duration in the south.

It must be noted that slavery is a necessary option that occurs as a physical phenomenon, inevitable, as a result of the economic basis of the south. thus may not be judged from morals, which is generally not helpful in understanding, modeling, and predicting $vb(x)$.

As a social factor stemming from the economic need for labor, the incoming servants would be materized, expressing in this case as European settlers convincing themselves the intrinsic inferiority of the slaves.

These forced labors may take in many forms, such as serfs, slaves, and servants, though share the characteritic of maximizing their utility as labor resource.

Without automated production of mechanical laborers, robots, human labarers remain as one of the most needed resource from the old world due to its difficult to produce locally, thereby resulting in the rapid scaling of trading of old world slaves for products of the new world. Assume effectiveness of slaves in the colonies and significant comparative advantage of products of the colonies, such trading would scale rapidly.

For example, the triangular trade involves New England, Africa, and West Indies. Traders bring rum from New England to Africa for slaves, then to West Indies for sugar and molasses, which is then shipped to New England for rum distillation. Feedback cycles involving the colonies and the old world cause rapid acceleration of the colonization process.

From the sickening cruelty of the transportation of Africans to North America, one may deduce that in the case of the colonization of the americas, scaling slaves per ship and number of ships is more efficient compared to ensuring the survival rate of each ship. As an economic inevitability, many slaves suffered and died.

Generally, $20%$ of the slaves are perished, economically acceptable. Those who survived are sold and may be categorized as skilled artisans (carpenters, blacksmiths, and bricklayers), producers of resources ($80-90%$), and logistical runners who help in sustaining the life of the colonizers by being servants in their family ($10-20%$).

This problem is no longer as relevant du jour as it is no longer economically efficient to transport coerced labor to other celestial objects and the cheaper alternative of automated machines.

In the case of the South, the population of the slaves may be as high as three times that of the white settlers. The slaves respond to their new condition in different ways. Most cope with it while preserving their tradition with baskets, music, and dance. The slaves' stress build up like a pressurized gas -- it keeps accumulating until explosion. Hence, it is not to the colonizers best interest to seal all stress reduction methods of the slaves. As a result, these traditions endured. Other slaves take on relatively direct resistance in attempts of reducing their workload by faking illness, organize slowdowns, and breaking tools. The rather extreme ones would "die before they can be conquer'd." Some colonizers sealed the container too well that there is no hole for the pressure to reduce while it is building up, leading to a revolution. One of the first attempts at an uprising is Stono Rebellion, began on a September Sunday in 1739. 20 slaves acquired arms and killed a few planter families and invited others to join. Despite their original members killed by suppression, this revolt sent a signal to both the slaves and the planters. The slaves realized revolting is possible as an option and it might succeed to the extent of killing some planters before dying; the planters realized the potential danger involved in using slaves as labors, most reacted by tightening the laws rather than relaxing them.
As a result, slave revolts continued to 1800s.

