== The North

physics:  Cold winters and rocky soil; abundance resources for fishing and lumbering; rivers not convient for ships

that restricted New Englanders to small farms.

As a result, the North played the role of trading the goods produced by the colonies to the mother nation and the old world. Due to the comparative advantage and small initial amount, the economy of the colonies grew rapidly, twice as fast as the British economy from 1650 to 1750.

The West Indies' demand of food and the North's comparative advantage in the production of a variety of livestock and crops matched and initiated the commerce economy of the North.

Diverse resources contribute to diverse economy. By 1760, the colonists built more than $1/3$ of British ships and produced more iron than England. A diverse economy, industries that require raw materials such as rum distillation and ship-building and significant comparative advantage cultivated the rise of the merchants, making them the most powerful group in the North. Trading centers and ports created urban centers. Philadelphia eventually became the sec-
ond largest city (after London) in the British empire. Philadelphia was the first
large city since ancient Roman times to be laid out on a gridlike street plan. This urban design has far-reaching consequences in American urban design in the future. Public infrastructures improved from other cities, with parks, streetlamps, police patrols, though water and firewood supply and garbage removal were lacking.

Size of the dwelling location indicates the social status of the occupant.

Compared to the South, the North has a more diverse population that introduced many conflicting interests. #image("/assets/image-2.png")

The early groups would likely resent the new groups sharing their resources, creating a cause of conflict.

Moreover, from the influx population, one can deduce that the people coming to the North are mostly single European males seeking economic oppurtunities while those to the South carry their families and wealth.

The North also used slavery for labor, though  raising wheat and corn did not require as much labor as raising tobacco or rice, so relatively less slavery; however, prejudices against slaves and freed blacks prevail like the South. Hence, one may deduce that the prejudices against populations of less economic status is not sensitive to their amount (method one of similar situation with minute differences). slaves in the North had greater legal standings, can appeal to the highest courts. Slave revolts also occur in the North, which faced just as harsh suppression as the South.

Similar to the South, women in the North had practically no legal nor finanacial rights and faced unceasing labor.

One particular event that demonstrate the strain in the Northern society caused by conflicting interests of groups, attacks of native Americans, religious fanaticism, and fear of slave uprising led to the buckling of rationality -- people accusing one another of practicing witchcraft, who in turn claim others are witches to prove that they themselves are not. Poor accused the rich. Dependent women turned to independent women.

This method of pointing to scapegoats for problems in the society is common throughout history and occur when the society is under stress, reflecting the "we lost to powerful witches, not our foolish self" mentality.

Despite material exchange, ideas transfers between the colony and the old world also play a significant role in shaping both sides. For example, enlightenment ideas traveled from Europe to the colonies and spread quickly in numerous books and pamphlets. Literacy was particularly high in New England
because the Puritans had long supported public education to ensure that every-one could read the Bible.

One outstanding Enlightenment figure was Benjamin Franklin. Franklin embraced the notion of obtaining truth through experimentation and reasoning. For example, his most famous experiment—flying a kite in a thunderstorm -- demonstrated that lightning was a form of electrical power.
